  "open_questions": [
    {
      "question": "The date rule's Date conversion spells a year outside 1000..9999 unpadded (999-06-15, 10000-01-01, -1-01-01), so its text stops ordering as a YYYY-MM-DD day. This PR routes a number through that same conversion (H3: a number and its Date agree), so a REST number now reaches the spelling too. os-dev rule 3 says a published defect this round touches must be fixed; the dispatch ruling says every non-number date comparand stays byte-identical, and fixing the spelling moves Date cells. Which governs?",
      "options": [
        "A — leave this PR as is (number ≡ Date on the whole Date range); file the spelling as its own class (a) card, whose ruling may move Date cells deliberately (4-digit pad for years 0..999; a decided reading for years past 9999 and below 0, which have no YYYY-MM-DD spelling at all).",
        "B — pad the year to 4 digits for numbers only in this PR: the number path becomes right for years 0..999, but a number and its Date disagree there (breaks H3), and years past 9999 / below 0 stay unresolved.",
        "C — fix the shared conversion here (pad 0..999) and move the Date cells, overriding the dispatch ruling; still leaves years past 9999 / below 0 without a pinned form."
      ],
      "recommendation": "A. Real business need: epoch-ms numbers before year 1000 or after 9999 on a date field have no measured writer; the measured pull is the card's 2026 numbers, which this PR serves. Long-term soundness: one conversion for Date and number (no third dialect); the unpadded spelling is a Date-arm defect, and its right fix (including the out-of-range years with no YYYY-MM-DD form) is a decision, not a mechanical edit, so the bounded-fix exemption's \"shape already pinned\" condition fails. Keeping AI from writing wrong code: B would teach two spellings of one day depending on input type, the exact dialect the single rule removed. Startup focus: no new surface; the edge gets its own card with evidence."
    }
  ],
